Author: Miller Lai <miller.lai@gmail.com>
Date:   Mon Aug 31 05:05:36 2026 -0400

    feat(cai): add the ticket backend seam and its GitHub implementation
    
    Unit 1 of the ticket-integration schedule. ticket_backend.py holds the three
    semantic capabilities a ticket system has to offer -- read a ticket, overwrite
    one comment in place, transition once -- behind an interface that never raises
    and never lets raw stderr reach a stored file. Only a closed set of category
    words leaves it, because the ledger it would otherwise reach is append-only and
    syncs to a cross-project file with no delete.
    
    Everything load-bearing on this path was measured rather than assumed:
    
    - the id `--json comments` returns is a GraphQL node id, and the REST PATCH
      endpoint rejects it; the numeric id it wants is parsed from the comment's own
      url. Feeding it the node id is the easiest mistake here
    - calls go through argv, never a shell: Git Bash rewrites an endpoint that
      starts with a slash into a filesystem path and the request fails
    - `gh issue close` on an already-closed issue exits 0, so transition_once is
      idempotent and safe to retry
    - the four stderr wordings classify() matches were captured from gh
    - a read never produces 403 -- GitHub answers an unreadable repo with "Could
      not resolve" so it does not leak existence -- so that category belongs to
      writes, where it doubles as the identity-change signal per DD10
    
    Comment find-back matches on marker AND author, because `--edit-last` targets
    "my last comment" rather than "the marked one"; with a human reply in between
    those are measurably different comments.
    
    25 tests, every one driven through a fake CLI via CAI_TICKET_CLI, so the suite
    runs on a machine with no gh installed.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

